From 6eedbde9fb1ccf0fadd022742dae7e6e0e994d78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: plotarmordev <299844489+plotarmordev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:58:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 54/54] drama: clip preparation tells two speakers apart by
 timbre, not pitch

Each stretch of speech between pauses gets its mean mel cepstra over 6 ms frames to 16 kHz; a short frame blurs the harmonics, so the cepstra follow the pitch less, though an octave jump still shows. The cepstra are whitened in the directions that vary by more than a millionth of the widest. A weighted two-means split, started from the stretch means' principal axis, puts the stretches in two groups, and the split sqrt(T^2 / rank), T^2 being the groups' two-sample Hotelling statistic over the rank whitened directions, is refused over 5.6. Groups that differ in a direction neither varies in, where the pooled covariance cannot measure the gap, are refused as two speakers rather than scored. The threshold is empirical, set on model-invented voices and synthetic mixes of them only, with margins of 0.73 and 0.80 on a small set. A take with fewer than two stretches of speech, or fewer than 12 measurable spectral directions (a repeated sound, for one), is refused instead of passed. Pitch spread is still reported but refuses nothing. Frame levels floor at -200 dB, so a take recorded quietly measures as a loud one does. The synthetic voices in the tests now have formants.
