 ## Unreleased
 
+- speech: `drama_voice_clip.py` tells two speakers apart by timbre instead of pitch, so an expressive single voice is no longer refused for its pitch range. A weighted two-means split puts its stretches of speech in two groups by their mel cepstra (6 ms frames to 16 kHz), and a split `sqrt(T² / rank)` over 5.6 is refused, T² being the groups' two-sample Hotelling statistic over the rank whitened directions; groups apart in a direction neither varies in are refused outright. The threshold is empirical, set on model-invented voices and synthetic mixes of them only: one voice measured up to 4.87 and two in a full-band take from 6.40, margins of 0.73 and 0.80 on a small set. A take with fewer than two stretches of speech, or a spectrum too degenerate to measure, is refused instead of passed. The report adds `speaker_split`; `pitch_spread_semitones` refuses nothing now ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: a voice file says whose voice it holds. `source` is `{kind: model-invented | owner | consented, note}`, the operator's attestation, and `--voices` refuses a file without one or with a blank note. `GET /v1/voices` lists the loaded voices (name, frames, seconds, source kind); there is no upload route. `tools/zig/drama_voice_clip.py` prepares a take: mono 44.1 kHz aimed at -20 dBFS speech level within -1 dBFS peaks, cut at a pause to a 10-30 s clip, refused when short, clipped, noisy (judged on the kept clip), over music or hum, or voiced by more than one speaker. Its transcript is either the take's, kept only when the cut leaves no loud frame out, or a local Whisper draft to correct; a confirmed transcript is never paired with a re-prepared clip. `drama_voice.py` encodes with `--reference` or `--native` when given, else with a `tensorfold-native` on `PATH` whose `--help` lists `voice encode` ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: `tensorfold-native voice encode CLIP.wav --model DIR --text T --name NAME --out NAME.json` writes a Drama 3 voice file with a native CUDA codec encoder (DAC encoder, pre_module and RVQ in BF16), no Python; on three of our test clips, 2.5 to 3 % of its semantic codes differ from the reference's ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md))
 - speech: the Drama 3 backbone's full attention runs the reference's softmax schedule: one running max per 64-key tile, with the reference kernel's exp, division, gate sigmoid and fused multiply-adds. On our GB10, the attention operator now matches the reference on 98.1% and 96.2% of its outputs at layers 3 and 39 (was 70.4% and 57.5%), and on our 182-token text fixture the backbone's distance to the reference fell from 0.124 to 0.053 relative L2 (semantic KL 0.024 to 0.0062, worst row cosine 0.80 to 0.92). Rows keep their bits whatever the chunk size, and decode uses the same arithmetic.
diff --git a/docs/drama-voices.md b/docs/drama-voices.md
index f88f2222..4675791f 100644
--- a/docs/drama-voices.md
+++ b/docs/drama-voices.md
@@ -29,9 +29,18 @@ The tool reads WAV, FLAC, Ogg and MP3, and writes 16-bit mono 44.1 kHz.
   - the kept clip, counted from its first loud frame to its last plus the 0.25 s edges, would be shorter than 10 s, or there is no pause to cut at. Pauses count toward that duration;
   - its background is less than 30 dB under the speech, on the whole take and again on the kept clip;
   - its pauses hold a steady tone, music or hum (an octave band from 100 Hz to 6.4 kHz with spectral flatness under 0.1);
-  - the median pitch of its stretches of speech spreads more than 6 semitones between the 20th and 80th percentile.
+  - its stretches of speech fall into two groups too far apart to be one voice (the speaker split, below), or it has fewer than two stretches to compare, or its spectrum varies too little to be measured.
 - **Limits.** The gates are cheap.
-  - The speaker check hears pitch only. It misses two speakers of a similar pitch, and a speaker who moves between very different registers can trip it.
+  - **Speaker split.** An empirically thresholded heuristic, not a calibrated test.
+    - Each stretch between pauses with at least 0.3 s of loud frames gets the mean of 36 mel cepstra, over 6 ms frames from 80 Hz to 16 kHz. A frame that short blurs the harmonics, so the cepstra follow the pitch less than a longer frame would; a jump of an octave still shows.
+    - The cepstra are whitened over the clip, in the directions that vary by more than a millionth of the widest. A take with fewer than 12 such directions is refused as unmeasurable; a repeated sound is one cause. Bandwidth alone does not decide it: synthetic speech low-passed at 2 kHz kept 27 directions.
+    - A weighted two-means split, started from the stretch means' principal axis, puts the stretches in two groups. With T² the two groups' two-sample Hotelling statistic (their mean difference over their pooled covariance) and rank the number of whitened directions, the split is `sqrt(T² / rank)`. A split over 5.6 is refused as two speakers.
+    - When the groups differ in a direction in which neither varies inside itself, the pooled covariance cannot measure that gap, and the take is refused as two speakers rather than scored.
+    - The groups are chosen from the same frames they are measured on, and neighbouring frames are correlated, so the split has no fixed chance level, and it grows with the number of frames. 5.6 is set by measurement only.
+    - The report gives `speaker_split`; `pitch_spread_semitones` is reported for reference and refuses nothing.
+  - Calibration, offline, on model-invented voices and synthetic mixes of them only: 17 single-voice clips, each whole, cut to 10-16 s and low-passed at 8 kHz, measured up to 4.87. Two-voice takes from four of those voices (one after the other, 3 s turns, 6 s each, a 3 s interjection) measured from 6.40 at full band. The margins are thin, 0.73 under 5.6 and 0.80 over it, and the set is small; a real recording of two people, or a more expressive single speaker, may land on the wrong side.
+  - It misses two speakers whose timbre is close in a band-limited take: low-passed at 8 kHz, 2 of 12 two-voice takes stayed under 5.6. It also misses a second voice with no stretch of its own (under 0.3 s between pauses, or speakers who overlap or hand over without a 0.15 s pause).
+  - It can refuse one voice whose timbre changes between stretches: a change of microphone or room within the take, whispering then shouting, or (on synthetic speech) a jump of an octave between sentences.
   - Music or noise more than 60 dB under the speech is not checked.
   - The noise floor is read from the quietest tenth of the kept clip, so a clip with almost no pauses reads as noisy.
 - **Transcript.** It must be the exact words of the clip as cut.
